        "question_text": "<p>In a hydraulic brake system, what principle explains why applying force to the brake pedal results in increased braking force at the wheels?</p>",
        "question_image": null,
        "answer_image": null,
        "explanation": "<p>The hydraulic brake system operates on <b>Pascal's Law</b>, which states that pressure applied to a confined fluid is transmitted equally in all directions. This allows a small force at the brake pedal to be multiplied into a larger force at the wheel cylinders or calipers.</p>",
